s problems, takes responsibility, but know-it-all, hot temper.\n\nWe need to decide whether to keep vote for A or shift. We could argue that B's stress handling is proven, and while B's nasty remarks are concerning, they may be mitigated. But crew cooperation is crucial. A unknown. Perhaps we should ask for more data on B's crew cooperation, C's stress resilience, D's stress resilience. But we need to give a recommendation now. Could we shift to B? Let's weigh: A strong technical, unknown stress and crew. B strong stress, conscientious, good weather, computer, but weak memory for numbers, nasty remarks. Memory for numbers could affect attention and accuracy. Nasty remarks affect crew cooperation. C: quick decisions, calm, tech, but communication difficulty, egocentric, not willing to further education. D: problem solving, responsibility, but hot temper, know-it-all.\n\nGiven safety-critical, crew cooperation and communication are vital. A's show-off and not open to new ideas may affect crew cooperation but less severe than nasty remarks or hot temper. However unknown stress resilience is a big gap. B's stress resilience is known, but nasty remarks are serious. Which is more critical? Probably crew cooperation is essential; nasty remarks could be fatal. A's show-off may be less severe. So maybe keep A.\n\nThus James Carter will reaffirm vote for A, but request more data on stress and crew for all candidates. Provide reasoning.\n\nNow produce PUBLIC_MESSAGE and METADATA_JSON with vote Candidate A."}
